SC BRIEF #204
Tuesday, September 8, 2026 | The morning read for supply-chain and logistics operators

Good morning. Customs explained tariff stacking, New York had a decent August, and we surveyed 200 shippers about their visibility platforms. Five items.

1. CBP clarifies how overlapping tariff programs stack
A CSMS message published Friday walks through the order of application when an entry is subject to more than one additional-duty program: Section 232, Section 301, and reciprocal tariffs. The examples matter because stacking order affects the dutiable base. Brokers are re-running August entries to check.

2. Port of New York and New Jersey: August volume up 2%
The port handled 745,000 TEUs in August, with rail lifts at ExpressRail up 8%. The Bayonne Bridge navigational-clearance work is complete; a 14,000-TEU vessel called at Port Newark last week.

3. Control-tower software: a reality check
Our survey of 200 shippers who bought "control tower" visibility platforms in the past three years found that data integration ate roughly 70% of project time and budget, and that only a third of respondents use the platform for decisions rather than reporting. The tools work; the data underneath them usually does not.

4. Bluewater Container Lines orders 12 methanol dual-fuel ships
Bluewater signed for twelve 16,000-TEU methanol-capable vessels for delivery from 2028. Green-methanol supply, not shipyard capacity, is the constraint.

5. Peregrine Air Cargo raises its Q3 outlook
Peregrine now sees Q3 cargo revenue up 9% year over year, citing e-commerce and semiconductor-equipment shipments out of Taiwan and Korea.

